{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:finite-approximations-without-compatibility","","<p><em> Every shared-prefix family\njust defined at depth <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> contains a\nfinite support <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and at a feedback row\nthis survives <em>both</em> outputs of the corpus’s total feedback\ntheorem; full-cylinder advance or a localized one-hole seam, which can\ndelete at most one of carries <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:exact-lebesgue-measure-dichotomy","Exact Lebesgue-measure dichotomy","<p><em> Either <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></math></span> for a finite\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>vol</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></mrow></msup></math></span>\nexactly, or <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mi>J</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></math></span> is infinite and the\nvolume is exactly <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:one-orbit","Stability of each fixed greedy\nprefix","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and,\nfor each fixed <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>, with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Apply the greedy rule\nwith target <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></math></span> and weights <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> in increasing order of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span>, through depths <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math></span>. For every fixed depth <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span>, the decisions at ranks <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></math></span> eventually agree with those of\nthe real half-greedy rule. This assertion concerns finite prefixes, not\nsurvival at all ranks.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:no-ties","No-ties lemma","<p><em> At every rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi></math></span> of the full greedy orbit for target\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, both defining comparisons are\nstrict: <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:tr-forced-greedy","Forced greedy","<p><em> For every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&gt;</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> for all <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi></math></span> (the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> terms agree exactly, and every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> tail term is strictly smaller than\nthe corresponding weight term). Consequently the greedy support is the\nunique candidate support, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>HalfRung</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> holds iff the greedy\norbit for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> under weights <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> never lands in a fatal interval\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Rank\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> is always a safe skip; ranks\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span> are always takes.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:tr-parity","Parity forces infinite support","<p><em> For\nevery <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, no finite <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> attains <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>HalfRung</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:tr-witness-exclusion","Witness exclusion","<p><em> Define the\nmisalignment mass </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi></mrow></munderover><mfrac><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>q</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span>,\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span>, and suppose some <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac></math></span>. Then no\nBoolean prefix <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> satisfies\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msubsup><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&lt;</mo><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>;</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This excludes the specified gap for\nevery Boolean prefix, not only the greedy prefix.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#cor:tr-half-lcm","Half-LCM horizon","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>lcm</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. For\nevery <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> there\nis an <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>15</mn></math></span>. Thus witness exclusion\nleaves only the finite window <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> to check.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:tr-mod12","Mod-12 filter, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span>","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>J</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></math></span>, the inequality <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>15</mn></math></span> implies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>12</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>M</mi></math></span>. Thus only multiples of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>12</mn></math></span> need be tested as witnesses in each\ninterval <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span>; no potentially\nuncovered rank is discarded.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:tr-finite-decision","Finite decision procedure","<p><em> Call\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> <strong>bad</strong>\nif no <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>J</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>11</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac></math></span>, and set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>:=</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>bad</mi></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>3</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Then\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>HalfRung</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> holds iff the\ngreedy orbit for <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> under weights\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></math></span> survives every rank from\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span> through <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>J</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. This is a finite exact decision\nprocedure.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:lower-bound-every-reset","A: a sufficient lower bound at every\nreset","<p><em> Assume that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>\nfor every upper or middle reset <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></math></span>. Then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Here is the role of the constants. Put </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></math></span>, one has <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>. A right branch at the\nfirst crossing of the largest omitted rank <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> would force <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, by tracing the\nexact affine recurrence back to that reset. The assumed strict lower\nbound excludes this crossing. The largest-skip induction then gives\nhalf-membership. The hypothesis concerns every actual reset beyond the\nthreshold; neither a long finite check nor an asserted run-length\nasymptotic supplies it.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:one-sided-finite-decision-boundary","C: the one-sided finite decision\nboundary","<p><em> The equivalence of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:greedy-survival-record\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:greedy-survival-record\">5.10</a>\nseparates two logically different kinds of evidence. A fatal greedy gap\nfound at a finite rank is a finite certificate that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. By contrast,\nmembership requires survival at every rank; this paper supplies no\nfinite certificate that the orbit survives forever and no completion\ntheorem turning a long surviving prefix into membership.</em></p>\n<p><em>The reset-crossing hypotheses, truncation-rung ladder, and\nsharp-capacity inequalities feed sufficient conditions for membership.\nThey are not proved equivalent to one another, nor is failure to find\none at a given depth evidence of survival. Consequently these searches\ngive at most a semi-decision procedure for <em>nonmembership of the\nspecific value <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span></em>. Halting\ndoes not prove the universal statement in #257, and non-halting does not\nprove that <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> is represented. In\nparticular, neither truth nor falsity of the universal problem is shown\nsemi-decidable here. Deeper negative search alone establishes nothing\nbeyond the tested finite ranks; this is the quantifier boundary of\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:one-sided\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:one-sided\">2.11</a>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:2adic-nogo","Centred completion of\nfixed-precision 2-adic data","<p><em> Fix <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and a finite list <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></math></span>, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> are integers and the integers\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></math></span> are odd. For every initial\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, there are integers\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></math></span> such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi></msup><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Thus one may\nprescribe the valuation and <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>u</mi></math></span> bits\nof the odd unit of each input coefficient while keeping every\n<em>successor</em> state in its stated centred interval. The initial\nstate is arbitrary.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:upper-unconditional","The upper (carries) successor needs\nno exceptional-cell exclusion","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></math></span> and suppose the upper branch occurs\nat row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi></math></span>, that is, the transition\nat row <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>s</mi></math></span> is on branch <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U</mi></mrow></math></span> of Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:dynamics\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:dynamics\">5.16</a>. Then </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Indeed, the\nreset identity expresses <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> as\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> plus a\nnonnegative reset charge. Thus the upper branch needs no\nexceptional-value exclusion for the next-row two-sided invariant. This\nconclusion concerns that induction step, not the absence of upper\ntransitions on the orbit.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:cd-neg3-impossible","<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> is impossible at the final middle\ntransition, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span>","<p><em> Consider\na hypothetical <em>final middle transition</em>: a middle transition at\nrow <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span> (<span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">¬</mi><mrow><mi>carries</mi></mrow></math></span>, and the\nmiddle-branch inequality <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>gap</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>belowPulse</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>terminalWeight</mi></mrow></math></span> holds at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span>), followed by an all-right tail forever\nafter (<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>seamGreedyWord</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>seamGreedyWord</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>extend</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>true</mi></mrow></math></span>).\nUnder these assumptions, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>belowPulse</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo>≤</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mn>4</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> i.e. <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> ; the cell <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> (and\nevery more negative value) is excluded.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Proof of the bound.</strong> The all-right-tail\nassumption puts the terminal-augmented finite prefix strictly above\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Consequently the producer\ncarry is below its complete incidence tail, or equivalently <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>seamWordFloorError</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>.\nThe lower bound uses the other endpoint: under the same tail assumption,\nthe finite value at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> plus the\ncomplete Mersenne tail remains below <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. The exact scaled remainder identity\ntherefore gives </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>4</mn><mi>D</mi></msup><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>e</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>D</mi></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em>\nThe last inequality follows by retaining the first two geometric\nchannels of the complete Mersenne tail. Put <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>seamWordFloorError</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. Pulse\nabsorption gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>belowPulse</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></math></span>, so </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mn>4</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>belowPulse</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>rem</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The left side is an\ninteger, hence is at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>, as\nrequired. The upper threshold inequality alone would not give this lower\nbound.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> This excludes <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> at a middle row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span> under the additional\nall-right-tail assumption. The general induction requires exclusion of\nall three cells at every middle row, not just under this extra\nhypothesis, together with its right-branch bound. The two statements\nshare the same coordinate <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></math></span> but\nhave different hypotheses. Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:cd-neg3-impossible\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:cd-neg3-impossible\">10.6</a> is universal\nover rows satisfying the additional all-right-tail assumption; it does\nnot assert that such a row exists. The induction hypothesis concerns\nevery middle row, without that tail assumption. Excluding <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> in the former statement therefore\ndoes not supply the exclusion required in the latter. <strong>Object or\nrepresentation:</strong> about the <em>object</em> under the stated\nhypothesis ; a genuine arithmetic consequence of the all-right-tail\nassumption via the fatal-gap orbit, not a coordinate artifact.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#cor:cd-remaining","Only <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> remain among the\nexceptional dyadic cells","<p><em> At a final middle row <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>13</mn></math></span>, Theorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:cd-neg3-impossible\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:cd-neg3-impossible\">10.6</a> removes\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></math></span> from the exceptional set <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, leaving <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> <em>within that set</em>. It does\nnot assert <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>:\nnonnegative values have not been excluded. Proposition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#prop:upper-unconditional\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:upper-unconditional\">10.5</a> handles\nupper transitions in the two-sided induction; it supplies no additional\nrestriction on a middle coordinate. For the all-middle-row induction,\nthe exclusion of all three values still needs proof without an\nall-right-tail assumption, together with the separate right-branch\ninequality.</em></p>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=d132333b629d"}
